question: should we use the decoder-adjoint (iterative) 2x downsampler when
  resampling is EXPLICITLY requested, instead of the sharper kernel libjxl
  v0.12 uses below effort 10?
harness: jxl-encoder/examples/resample_kernel_cost (best of 5, warmed)
method: both round trips do one downsample plus one IDENTICAL upsample, so the
  difference between them is exactly the extra downsample cost the encoder
  would pay. Encode time is a real encode at effort 7 with resampling 2.

COST (this file): extra downsample ~700 ms per megapixel, which is ~6.5x our
  entire effort-7 encode (112 ms at 1 MP). Our resampling is fully scalar --
  144 taps per output pixel for sharper, plus 3 adjoint-gradient rounds over
  the full-resolution image for iterative -- with no SIMD anywhere in
  vardct/resampling.rs, so this is an UNOPTIMIZED price.
  For context, cjxl v0.12 on the same image: e7 (sharper) 0.086 s, e10
  (iterative + other kGlacier work) 0.862 s = 10x. So the kernel is expensive
  in libjxl too; we are not anomalously slow. libjxl's own note in
  enc_frame.cc quotes "80% slowdown", which its e7-vs-e10 wall time does not
  obviously support.

QUALITY (from benchmarks/resample_admissibility_2026-09-06.images.tsv, 84
  image x size cells): iterative lowers the 2x floor by a median 11.2%
  butteraugli, mean 11.5%, p90 24.4%, best 32.4% -- but it is WORSE on 7 of 84
  cells (worst -11.9%), and the epa-report stratum is negative overall
  (-3.8% median). So it is "usually better, occasionally worse", not
  unconditionally better.

VERDICT: do not blanket-flip the gate. (a) the win is not universal, so a
  blanket flip regresses some content; (b) the cost is ~6.5x the encode on
  code that has no SIMD at all, so we would be paying an unoptimized price;
  (c) it is a deliberate divergence from v0.12, which is the reference we are
  directed to match. Sequence if pursued: SIMD the kernels first (archmage /
  magetypes per the workspace rules), re-measure, then consider a keep-best
  gate that runs both and keeps the better result, rather than a blanket flip.
  Low priority regardless: the default never resamples, so this only affects
  callers who explicitly ask for with_resampling(N) at effort <= 9.

REUSE STUDY (2026-09-06): can zenyuv / zenresize / sharpyuv supply this?

zenyuv (already an optional dep of this crate, feature `chroma-subsampling`)
  ships Sharp YUV: an L2-optimal Newton-step refinement of Cb/Cr on the 4:2:0
  grid, with AVX2/NEON/WASM SIMD. Same FAMILY as the iterative downsampler --
  refine the downsampled data so the decoder's upsample reconstructs better --
  but the SOLVER DOES NOT TRANSFER. zenyuv's model upsamples chroma
  nearest-neighbour from the 4:2:0 grid (`sharp.rs`: "nearest-neighbor to the
  co-sited position"), which makes every 2x2 block INDEPENDENT; that
  independence is the entire reason a closed-form per-block Newton step is
  valid and fast. JXL's decoder upsampler is a 5x5 per-phase kernel, so one
  downsampled pixel affects a 5x5 neighbourhood of outputs and neighbouring
  blocks are COUPLED. A per-block Newton step is simply not correct there,
  which is why libjxl solves it as 3 global adjoint-gradient rounds instead.
  Not copy-pasteable; the shared part is the idea, not the code.

zenresize is a separable-filter image resizer (`resize_rgba8`, filter kernels,
  SIMD). Two reasons it does not apply: (1) the JXL sharper kernel is NOT
  separable, and it fails on plain ACCURACY, long before any bit-exactness
  policy is reached -- measured max abs error of a rank-K approximation
  against the full 2D kernel on a real 512x512 plane:
      rank 1 (24 taps, 6x cheaper): 2.46e-01   catastrophic
      rank 2 (48 taps, 3x cheaper): 1.47e-01   catastrophic
      rank 3 (72 taps, 2x cheaper): 5.23e-03   visible (~1.3/255)
      rank 4 (96 taps, 1.5x)      : 4.62e-03   visible
      rank 6 (144 taps, no saving): 2.66e-15   exact
  The "99.30 % rank-1 energy" figure is a TRAP: energy is dominated by the
  central positive lobe, while the sharpening behaviour lives in the small
  negative lobes (-0.163, -0.017) that a low-rank fit destroys first. You must
  reach essentially full rank to be correct, at which point there is no saving.
  (2) the iterative step is not a resize at all, it is an inverse problem
  against a specific upsampler; zenresize carries no adjoint machinery.

IS BIT-EXACTNESS WITH libjxl ACTUALLY REQUIRED? Checked rather than assumed:
  - The Libjxl BYTE-LOCK never reached the resampling path. Its 10 cells sit at
    d in {0.5, 1, 2, 4} and auto-resample fires at d >= 10, so the
    `EncoderStrategy::Libjxl` parity CLAIM covered resampled encodes while no
    test did. That gap is now closed by an 11th cell
    (`gradient_rgb_64x64_e7_d12_resampled`).
  - The 7 `_r*_` hash-lock cells pin OUR output for determinism, not libjxl
    parity; they are regenerable.
  So bit-exactness is not demanded by any pre-existing test. It is worth having
  for three reasons: it is the oracle that caught the f32::MIN defect; it backs
  the Libjxl-parity claim at d >= 10 that is now actually tested; and strict
  summation order keeps results identical across ISAs, avoiding more per-arch
  hash-lock variants (issue #70). Crucially it costs nothing here: padding +
  vectorising ACROSS OUTPUT PIXELS keeps each pixel's 144-tap order unchanged,
  so the fast path stays bit-exact. Bit-exactness never had to be traded away;
  separability was ruled out by accuracy alone.

sharpyuv (libwebp) is the same family as zenyuv and is also foreign codec
  software, which the workspace rules exclude from imazen pipelines anyway.

WHERE THE SPEED ACTUALLY IS: the current scalar kernel runs at 0.44 GFLOP/s
  (0.26 MP out x 144 taps = 75 MFLOP in 173 ms), 20-100x off a single core's
  scalar peak. The inner loop calls `clamp_idx` PER TAP -- 144 edge clamps per
  output pixel -- which is exactly what this repo's own "Buffer Padding Rule"
  forbids ("pad and align buffers upfront with edge replication rather than
  adding bounds checks throughout"). Pad the plane once with edge replication,
  drop the clamp from the interior loop, then vectorise ACROSS OUTPUT PIXELS
  (one lane per output pixel, tap order unchanged per pixel) so the float
  summation order is preserved and the libjxl bit-exactness lock still holds.
  That is the cheap, correctness-preserving win; do it before revisiting the
  kernel-choice policy, because today keep-best would double a cost that is
  already 6.5x the encode.
